\details{
\strong{The fast response.} The rise in flow from one step to the next is moved almost only by
quick runoff, so the correlation of a gauge's rain with the rise a few steps later measures
how well that gauge explains the spikes. \code{lag_h} is the lag of the strongest
correlation, \code{r} its value.

\strong{Why not the slow response.} Baseflow answers to the rain of many storms over days or
weeks, and over such spans the gauges' totals converge: a correlation of gauge rain with
baseflow comes out much the same for every gauge, whichever drives it. Long-term drift
between gauges is a matter for the double-mass curve of \code{\link[=water_balance]{water_balance()}}.

\strong{Flood by flood.} For each of the \code{floods} largest events found on the catchment rain, by
peak flow, each gauge's rain is
correlated with the rise in flow across the event, at that gauge's own fast lag; the gauge
with the highest correlation wins the event. \code{event_wins} counts the wins, \code{event_share}
gives them as a share of the events with a winner. \code{size_r} is the correlation, across
events, of a gauge's event rain with the event's quick flow: whether the gauge sees the big
storms as big. \code{dry_events} counts events where a gauge recorded under a tenth of the
median gauge, when that median is at least \code{dry_mm}: a funnel blocked, a logger down, or a
storm that missed the gauge. A storm over one gauge alone leaves the median low and flags
nobody.

\strong{Reading it.} A gauge with a clearly higher \code{r} and most of the wins drives the
spike. Rain cannot follow the flow it causes, so a negative best lag means the gauge's clock
is late, or the flow's early; a gauge whose lag differs from its neighbours' by a whole
number of hours points the same way. A gauge with no wins, a low \code{size_r} or
many dry events is worth checking against its neighbours and the map. Correlations
between rain gauges blur the differences, as they do in any cross-correlation analysis
(Larocque et al., 1998): read the ranking and the gaps between gauges, not the values
alone.
